### Docker Dev Container

Docker builds in two stages: `dev` (base packages, LaTeX, the package
manager, [herdr](https://herdr.dev)'s terminal workspace manager — no agent
CLI) and `harness`, which layers Claude Code / Antigravity on top of `dev`.
This split means the container works two different ways depending on
whether you have the `.friday/` submodule checked out.

**With the submodule, after running `init_harness.py`:** setup wrote
`COMPOSE_FILE=docker/docker-compose.yml:docker/docker-compose.harness.yml`
into the gitignored root `.env`, so plain `docker compose` commands pick up
both files (the base `dev` service plus the harness override — agent config
volumes, `target: harness`) automatically:

```bash
docker compose build            # build the image
docker compose up -d            # start the container, detached
docker compose attach harness   # attach to the container's shell
# or, from another terminal (independent of the container's main process):
docker compose exec harness bash
docker compose down             # stop it (project dir untouched)
```

**Without the submodule** (e.g. a teammate who cloned without
`--recurse-submodules`, or never ran setup): there is no root `.env`, so
Compose falls back to just the base file and gets a working `dev` container
with no agent tooling on it. Point Compose at it explicitly with `-f`:

```bash
docker compose -f docker/docker-compose.yml up -d
```

Either way, the bind-mounted project directory is your host filesystem,
mounted at `/[SET AT SETUP: PROJECT_NAME_LOWER]`; named volumes keep auth
and caches across restarts. The image follows this project's own
`harness.config.env` (package manager, agent CLI, LaTeX, GPU) — after
re-running `init_harness.py` to reconfigure, run `build` again to pick up a
changed `docker/Dockerfile`. The container starts a plain shell; herdr is
installed unconditionally — run `herdr` by hand from that shell when you
want it. See `.friday/reference/docker.md` for the full
workflow, including reconfiguring mounts.
